WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.860
JISHNU DAS: I'm Jishnu Das, and I'm
a researcher here at the World Bank.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:11.880
The World Bank is a multilateral
financial institution.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.120
What that means is it's a
group of different countries

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.860
who got together and said, one way we
can help fight poverty around the world

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.430
is to pool our money together
and either lend or give grants

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.880
to poorer countries on various
programs and various ways of helping

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.320
them to improve the living standards
of the people in those countries.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.130
Our role is always to represent
people, particularly poor people

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:38.160
in these countries, and talk a lot
about what their experience is,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.440
what the kind of problems
they face are, and what

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.240
are the kind of programs that
can help improve their lives.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.020
I work mostly on health and
education, and I love the work I do.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.360
I think the part that I most
enjoy is actually learning

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.090
from the populations we work with.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.660
In 1998, I actually set up a clinic
with my brother, who's a cardiologist,

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.781
in a mountain village in India.

00:00:59.781 --> 00:01:02.280
And I think one of the things
that we were very surprised by

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.400
was there were many, many health care
providers around, which kind of went

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.160
against this idea that the biggest
problem in low-income countries

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.490
is a shortage of health care providers.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:15.597
Let's always keep in mind that there's
enormous variation across the world.

00:01:15.597 --> 00:01:17.930
And I think that definitely
parts of sub-Saharan Africa,

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.920
for instance, where access is
an issue, particularly they're

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.410
talking about the belt of
the Sahara in western Africa,

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:26.370
so Chad, Togo, Benin, Niger, you
know, Burkina Faso, I think, access

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:27.160
is a huge problem.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.090
But for a lot of the
other world, it's not.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.570
So if you think about
India, the average village

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.980
has about n health care providers in it.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:38.760
Then you say, well,
look, it's not access

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.270
we are talking about because after all,
who are these health care providers.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:47.010
What we are talking about access is
guys who are fully qualified doctors.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.560
Then you say, well, of
course, access is an issue

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.690
because look, fully qualified doctors,
there's only one every five villages.

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:56.070
Then they come back to
you and say, don't you

00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:00.600
have the idea that a fully qualified
doctor is a high quality doctor.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.870
And the moment you say, yes, I have
you because then I can say, well,

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:05.670
do you have any evidence for that?

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.381
And that's when I bring forth
to say, a fully qualified doctor

00:02:08.381 --> 00:02:10.380
in the public sector is
worse than the guy who's

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.870
not qualified in the private sector.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:18.570
So I think people always have in the
back of their mind access with quality.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:21.000
But the mistake that's
being made time and again

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.850
is they equate
qualifications with quality.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:27.470
And once we can break that mental
model, I think we're through.

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:29.470
I think we are all talking
about the same thing.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.600
It is just semantics.

